$DATUM 108-88-3
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM 23. Using a modification of procedures reported by Denmark,17 in a
nitrogen filled glovebox, a 4 mL vial equipped with a stirbar and a
Teflon lined septum cap was charged with tetrabutylammnium fluoride
trihydrate (315 mg, 1 mmol). The sealed vial was brought out of the
glovebox and was connected to nitrogen via a double manifold. Toluene
(700 μL) was added to the vial via syringe with stirring followed by H2O
(90 μL, 5 mmol), which was added via syringe to form a solution of
tetrabutylammonium fluoride octahydrate. A hot 15 mL high pressure
reaction vessel equipped with a stir bar and a Teflon valve was attached
to a double manifold and was allowed to cool to room temperature under
vacuum. The vessel was backfilled with nitrogen, opened to air, and
2-dicyclohexylphosphino-2’-4’-6’-triisopropylbiphenyl (XPhos, 53 mg, 0.11
mmol) and Pd(dba)2 (29 mg, 0.05 mmol) were added. The Teflon valve was
then replaced, and the vessel was evacuated and backfilled with nitrogen
three times. Next, under positive nitrogen pressure, the cap was then
replaced with a septum. The solution of tetrabutylammonium fluoride
octahydrate was transferred to the high pressure reaction vessel via
syringe, with stirring, and the vial was rinsed with toluene (300 μL)
which was also added to the high pressure reaction vessel via syringe.
The mixture was stirred for 25 minutes before the silane (5a: 118 mg or
5b: 125 mg, 0.5mmol) and phenyl trifluoromethanesulfonate (120 μL, 0.75
mmol) were sequentially added, each in one portion, via syringe with
stirring. The Teflon valve was replaced under positive nitrogen pressure,
and the reaction vessel was sealed. The vessel was then heated at 90 ºC
in an oil bath with stirring for 16 h, after which time the reaction was
cooled to rt. The reaction vessel was then opened to air, and
approximately 5 mL of brine and 10 mL of diethyl ether were added. The
organic layer was removed, and the brine was extracted twice more with 10
mL portions of diethyl ether. The combined organic layers were dried
(MgSO4), filtered, and concentrated in vacuo. The product was purified
using flash silica chromatography (0-10% CH2Cl2:hexanes) and concentrated
in vacuo. 100 mg (95%, from 5a) of a white solid.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM silyl-Heck reaction, sealed vessel used, alternative preparation shown,
Hiyama-Denmark reaction
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Preparation of Vinyl Silyl Ethers and Disiloxanes via the Silyl-Heck
Reaction of Silyl Ditriflates
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Martin, Sara E. S.; Watson, Donald A.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Journal of the American Chemical Society (2013), 135(36), 13330-13333

